Playwright ARIA extractor
=========================

Tri-agent workflow
------------------

The retrieval workflow has three model-driven roles:

- `InstructorAgent` extracts the literal seed URL, goal, context terms,
  Controller objective, and success criteria. It coordinates the full run.
- `ControllerAgent` owns a persistent `AriaPage` and selects bounded Playwright
  actions using current ARIA observations.
- `BuilderAgent` converts the final ARIA evidence into content and layout
  knowledge-graph triples.

Create all three with one LangChain-compatible model:

    from agents import create_tri_agent

    with create_tri_agent("ollama:llama3.2", max_controller_actions=5) as instructor:
        result = instructor.invoke(
            "Find the recipe time at https://example.com/recipe"
        )

    print(result.plan)
    print(result.controller.observations)
    print(result.graph.triples)
    print(result.metrics)

Different LangChain chat models can be supplied to each role:

    instructor = create_tri_agent(
        model="ollama:llama3.2",
        instructor_model=my_planning_model,
        controller_model=my_navigation_model,
        builder_model=my_graph_model,
    )

Run the command-line entry point with a request containing a literal seed URL:

    python main.py "Find the recipe time at https://example.com/recipe"

Every output is a Pydantic model. Controller observations preserve the selected
action, URL, page title, ARIA snapshot, detected roles/properties, errors, and
duration. Graph triples are marked as either `content` or `layout` and include
an ARIA evidence excerpt.

Install the Python dependency and Chromium browser:

    pip install -r requirements.txt
    playwright install chromium

Use the extractor:

    from utils.aria import extract

    aria = extract("https://example.com")
    print(aria)

The function opens the URL in headless Chromium and returns the page body's
ARIA snapshot as a string.

For interactive inspection, keep the browser open with `AriaPage`:

    from utils.aria import AriaPage

    with AriaPage(headless=False) as aria_page:
        aria_page.navigate("https://example.com")
        print(aria_page.aria)
        print(aria_page.find("button"))

        # Use Playwright directly through the exposed page or locator helper.
        print(aria_page.page.title())
        print(aria_page.locator("a").count())

        # Regenerate ARIA for only part of the page after interacting with it.
        aria_page.locator("button").first.click()
        print(aria_page.snapshot("main"))

Filter unwanted ARIA information when constructing the object or for one
snapshot:

    with AriaPage(
        exclude_roles={"navigation", "banner", "img"},
        exclude_patterns={r"cookie", r"advertisement"},
        exclude_properties={"level", "disabled"},
    ) as aria_page:
        aria_page.navigate("https://example.com")
        print(aria_page.aria)      # filtered
        print(aria_page.raw_aria)  # original Playwright output

Roles and matching lines remove their complete child subtrees. Properties only
remove bracketed attributes. Existing output can be filtered again without
navigating:

    reduced = aria_page.filter(
        aria_page.raw_aria,
        exclude_roles={"link"},
    )

Inspect the roles and properties present in the raw snapshot, and return the
lines matched by candidate patterns:

    print(aria_page.roles())
    print(aria_page.properties())
    print(aria_page.pattern_matches({r"cookie", r"advertisement"}))

Return everything as one serializable dictionary:

    details = aria_page.describe(patterns={r"cookie", r"advertisement"})
    print(details)

The one-shot function accepts the same filters:

    aria = extract(
        "https://example.com",
        exclude_roles={"navigation", "img"},
        exclude_patterns={r"cookie"},
    )
